<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">2011 Tōhoku earthquake and tsunami</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2011">2011</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as Great East Japan Earthquake, Higashi Nihon Daishinsai, 3.11</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tohoku-region">Tōhoku</a> earthquake and tsunami was a magnitude 9.0–9.1 undersea megathrust earthquake that struck on 11 March 2011 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>, 72 km east of the Oshika Peninsula of the Tōhoku region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and the tsunami it caused. It was the most powerful earthquake ever recorded in Japan, and its waves may have reached 40.5 m at Miyako and travelled up to 10 km inland in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sendai">Sendai</a> area. Official figures released in 2021 reported 19,759 deaths, 6,242 injured and 2,553 missing. The tsunami caused the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear disaster, with meltdowns of three reactors, and the economic damage, estimated at over $300 billion, made it the costliest natural disaster in history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/2011-tohoku-earthquake-and-tsunami.jpg" alt="2011 Tōhoku earthquake and tsunami" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Navy photo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SH-60B_helicopter_flies_over_Sendai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
